            <ArticleTitle>Chronic progressive polyarthritis in a domestic shorthair cat.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>621-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 6-year-old, neutered male, domestic shorthair cat was presented with shifting leg lameness and palpable effusion of the carpal and tarsal joints. Blood work, arthrocentesis, and radiographs identified an immune-mediated erosive polyarthritis. The cat was positive for feline syncytia-forming virus, and with his signalment, was diagnosed with feline chronic progressive polyarthritis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
